{"title":"Micah","markdown":{"yaml":{"title":"Micah"},"headingText":"Micah","containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n\n\n::: {.book-summary}\n## Book Summary Insight\n\nMicah alternates between judgment and hope while announcing the coming Messiah from Bethlehem. The book teaches that God desires justice, mercy, and humble fellowship with Him.\n:::\n\n::: {.chapter-entry}\n## Chapter 1\n\n<div class=\"key-verse\">Micah 1:2</div>\n\n<div class=\"identifier\">Hear, all ye people</div>\n\n### Why This Verse\n\nOpening prophetic summons\n\n### Chapter Insight\n\nThis chapter announces God's judgment against Samaria and Jerusalem. The key verse emphasizes that God sees and responds to the sins of His people.\n:::\n\n::: {.chapter-entry}\n## Chapter 2\n\n<div class=\"key-verse\">Micah 2:13</div>\n\n<div class=\"identifier\">The breaker is come up before them</div>\n\n### Why This Verse\n\nMessianic interpretation\n\n### Chapter Insight\n\nThis chapter condemns oppression, greed, and exploitation. The key verse highlights God's concern for justice and the protection of the vulnerable.\n:::\n\n::: {.chapter-entry}\n## Chapter 3\n\n<div class=\"key-verse\">Micah 3:8</div>\n\n<div class=\"identifier\">I am full of power by the Spirit of the LORD</div>\n\n### Why This Verse\n\nProphetic authority\n\n### Chapter Insight\n\nThis chapter rebukes corrupt leaders, priests, and prophets. The key verse demonstrates that leadership without righteousness invites God's judgment.\n:::\n\n::: {.chapter-entry}\n## Chapter 4\n\n<div class=\"key-verse\">Micah 4:3</div>\n\n<div class=\"identifier\">They shall beat their swords into plowshares</div>\n\n### Why This Verse\n\nFamous peace prophecy\n\n### Chapter Insight\n\nThis chapter promises future peace and restoration under God's reign. The key verse envisions the nations coming to the Lord and learning His ways.\n:::\n\n::: {.chapter-entry}\n## Chapter 5\n\n<div class=\"key-verse\">Micah 5:2</div>\n\n<div class=\"identifier\">Bethlehem Ephratah</div>\n\n### Why This Verse\n\nMessianic prophecy quoted in Matthew\n\n### Chapter Insight\n\nThis chapter foretells the coming ruler from Bethlehem. The key verse points to the Messiah who will shepherd His people in peace.\n:::\n\n::: {.chapter-entry}\n## Chapter 6\n\n<div class=\"key-verse\">Micah 6:8</div>\n\n<div class=\"identifier\">What doth the LORD require of thee?</div>\n\n### Why This Verse\n\nOne of the most quoted verses in the OT\n\n### Chapter Insight\n\nThis chapter presents God's case against His people and calls for genuine obedience. The key verse summarizes God's desire for justice, mercy, and humble fellowship with Him.\n:::\n\n::: {.chapter-entry}\n## Chapter 7\n\n<div class=\"key-verse\">Micah 7:8</div>\n\n<div class=\"identifier\">Rejoice not against me, O mine enemy</div>\n\n### Why This Verse\n\nHope after failure\n\n### Chapter Insight\n\nThis chapter moves from lament over sin to confidence in God's forgiveness and restoration. The key verse celebrates God's unmatched mercy toward repentant people.\n:::\n\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":"\n\n# Micah\n\n::: {.book-summary}\n## Book Summary Insight\n\nMicah alternates between judgment and hope while announcing the coming Messiah from Bethlehem. The book teaches that God desires justice, mercy, and humble fellowship with Him.\n:::\n\n::: {.chapter-entry}\n## Chapter 1\n\n<div class=\"key-verse\">Micah 1:2</div>\n\n<div class=\"identifier\">Hear, all ye people</div>\n\n### Why This Verse\n\nOpening prophetic summons\n\n### Chapter Insight\n\nThis chapter announces God's judgment against Samaria and Jerusalem. The key verse emphasizes that God sees and responds to the sins of His people.\n:::\n\n::: {.chapter-entry}\n## Chapter 2\n\n<div class=\"key-verse\">Micah 2:13</div>\n\n<div class=\"identifier\">The breaker is come up before them</div>\n\n### Why This Verse\n\nMessianic interpretation\n\n### Chapter Insight\n\nThis chapter condemns oppression, greed, and exploitation. The key verse highlights God's concern for justice and the protection of the vulnerable.\n:::\n\n::: {.chapter-entry}\n## Chapter 3\n\n<div class=\"key-verse\">Micah 3:8</div>\n\n<div class=\"identifier\">I am full of power by the Spirit of the LORD</div>\n\n### Why This Verse\n\nProphetic authority\n\n### Chapter Insight\n\nThis chapter rebukes corrupt leaders, priests, and prophets. The key verse demonstrates that leadership without righteousness invites God's judgment.\n:::\n\n::: {.chapter-entry}\n## Chapter 4\n\n<div class=\"key-verse\">Micah 4:3</div>\n\n<div class=\"identifier\">They shall beat their swords into plowshares</div>\n\n### Why This Verse\n\nFamous peace prophecy\n\n### Chapter Insight\n\nThis chapter promises future peace and restoration under God's reign. The key verse envisions the nations coming to the Lord and learning His ways.\n:::\n\n::: {.chapter-entry}\n## Chapter 5\n\n<div class=\"key-verse\">Micah 5:2</div>\n\n<div class=\"identifier\">Bethlehem Ephratah</div>\n\n### Why This Verse\n\nMessianic prophecy quoted in Matthew\n\n### Chapter Insight\n\nThis chapter foretells the coming ruler from Bethlehem. The key verse points to the Messiah who will shepherd His people in peace.\n:::\n\n::: {.chapter-entry}\n## Chapter 6\n\n<div class=\"key-verse\">Micah 6:8</div>\n\n<div class=\"identifier\">What doth the LORD require of thee?</div>\n\n### Why This Verse\n\nOne of the most quoted verses in the OT\n\n### Chapter Insight\n\nThis chapter presents God's case against His people and calls for genuine obedience. The key verse summarizes God's desire for justice, mercy, and humble fellowship with Him.\n:::\n\n::: {.chapter-entry}\n## Chapter 7\n\n<div class=\"key-verse\">Micah 7:8</div>\n\n<div class=\"identifier\">Rejoice not against me, O mine enemy</div>\n\n### Why This Verse\n\nHope after failure\n\n### Chapter Insight\n\nThis chapter moves from lament over sin to confidence in God's forgiveness and restoration. The key verse celebrates God's unmatched mercy toward repentant people.\n:::\n\n"},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":"auto","echo":true,"output":true,"warning":true,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"html","fig-align":"default","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":"none","code-overflow":"scroll","code-link":false,"code-line-numbers":false,"code-tools":false,"tbl-colwidths":"auto","merge-includes":true,"inline-includes":false,"preserve-yaml":false,"latex-auto-mk":true,"latex-auto-install":true,"latex-clean":true,"latex-min-runs":1,"latex-max-runs":10,"latex-makeindex":"makeindex","latex-makeindex-opts":[],"latex-tlmgr-opts":[],"latex-input-paths":[],"latex-output-dir":null,"link-external-icon":false,"link-external-newwindow":false,"self-contained-math":false,"format-resources":[],"notebook-links":true},"pandoc":{"standalone":true,"wrap":"none","default-image-extension":"png","to":"html","css":["../../styles/bible.css"],"toc":true,"toc-depth":3,"number-sections":false,"output-file":"micah.html"},"language":{"toc-title-document":"Table of contents","toc-title-website":"On this page","related-formats-title":"Other Formats","related-notebooks-title":"Notebooks","source-notebooks-prefix":"Source","other-links-title":"Other Links","code-links-title":"Code Links","launch-dev-container-title":"Launch Dev Container","launch-binder-title":"Launch Binder","article-notebook-label":"Article Notebook","notebook-preview-download":"Download Notebook","notebook-preview-download-src":"Download Source","noteb